": ["past"]}, {"word": "swomp", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "To drench or fill with water.", "tags": [], "examples": ["The boat was swamped in the storm."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "cs", "name": "Czech"}, "word": "zaplavit"}, {"language": {"code": "cs", "name": "Czech"}, "word": "zatopit"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(figurative) To overwhelm; to make too busy, or overrun the capacity of.", "tags": ["figuratively"], "examples": ["I have been swamped with paperwork ever since they started using the new system."], "quotes": [{"text": "\"I'm being swamped now with calls from parents and young people who are upset that there are not many options for employment this summer,\" he said.", "reference": "1991 May 5, Stewart Ain, “Bottom Falls Out Of Summer Job Market”, in The New York Times, →ISSN:"}, {"text": "Mr. Spitzer’s defeat of his Democratic opponent […] ended a primary season in which Hillary Rodham Clinton swamped an antiwar challenger for renomination to the Senate.", "reference": "2006 September 13, Patrick Healy, “Spitzer and Clinton Win in N.Y. Primary”, in The New York Times, →ISSN:"}, {"text": "It was only the outstanding Cech that stood between Arsenal and a second-half rout as Spurs simply swamped their opponents after the break with a formidable display of power, pace and sheer intensity.", "reference": "2018 February 10, Phil McNulty, “Tottenham Hotspur 1 - 0 Arsenal”, in BBC:"}, {"text": "All is well until Treorchy, where the platform is swamped by teenagers who have been attending an event. Around four dozen unescorted 12 to 16 year-olds swarm aboard and begin to run riot through the train. Their behaviour is appalling and the presence of CCTV no deterrent.", "reference": "2024 November 13, Paul Bigland, “Much to admire... but pockets of neglect”, in RAIL, number 1022, page 48:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code